(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-hemostasis-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Hemostasis — Quiz 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Albumin, the most abundant plasma protein, is important because it...","choices":["forms the fibrin meshwork that seals a damaged vessel as it is usually described here","carries oxygen from the lungs to the peripheral tissues","generates most of the plasma colloid osmotic pressure and transports many substances","acts as an antibody that neutralizes invading microbes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Albumin is the chief contributor to plasma colloid (oncotic) osmotic pressure, keeping fluid within the vasculature, and it serves as a carrier for hormones, fatty acids, drugs, and other poorly soluble substances. Low albumin therefore causes edema. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-5"},{"q":"Blood serves three broad roles — transport, regulation, and protection. Which pairing correctly matches a component to one of these roles?","choices":["Platelets transport dietary nutrients, while the red cells are what defend against infection","White cells regulate body temperature through inflammatory signaling, while plasma proteins carry oxygen","Plasma dissolves most oxygen for transport, while the red cells are what actually clot the blood","Red cells carry out transport of oxygen, while platelets and clotting proteins provide protection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Red cells (via hemoglobin) exemplify the transport role by carrying oxygen, while platelets and plasma clotting proteins serve the protective role by sealing damaged vessels; each component maps to a distinct one of blood's broad functions. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"Plasma differs from serum in that plasma still contains...","choices":["the clotting proteins such as fibrinogen that serum has lost","antibodies that are absent from serum as it typically occurs here","dissolved oxygen that serum cannot hold","far more red blood cells than serum does"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Plasma is the fluid obtained when clotting is prevented, so it retains fibrinogen and the other clotting factors; serum is the fluid left after blood has clotted, meaning those clotting proteins have been consumed and removed. That is the defining difference between the two. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-5"},{"q":"When a blood sample is centrifuged, the thin 'buffy coat' between the packed red cells and the plasma consists of...","choices":["white blood cells and platelets","clotting factors that have precipitated out","concentrated plasma proteins only","immature red blood cells released early"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Centrifuged blood separates into dense red cells at the bottom, plasma on top, and a thin intermediate buffy coat containing the white blood cells and platelets, which are intermediate in density between red cells and plasma. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"Which best describes a functional distinction between plasma proteins and the formed elements of blood?","choices":["Plasma proteins buffer carbon dioxide, while the formed elements dissolve dietary lipids","Plasma proteins carry oxygen, while the formed elements maintain colloid osmotic pressure","Plasma proteins clot the blood on their own, while the formed elements neutralize all invading toxins","Plasma proteins such as albumin generate colloid osmotic pressure, while red cells transport oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and the other plasma proteins are too large to cross the capillary wall freely, so they hold water in the vasculature and create colloid (oncotic) osmotic pressure; the formed element responsible for oxygen carriage is the red cell, via hemoglobin. The two therefore serve genuinely different roles. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-6"},{"q":"Each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 mL of O2. Why does anemia lower oxygen delivery even when arterial PO2 is normal?","choices":["Less hemoglobin is available to bind oxygen, so the oxygen content per volume of blood falls","Fewer red cells increase plasma viscosity, which physically traps the oxygen under these conditions","Anemia saturates the remaining hemoglobin so tightly that it cannot release oxygen","Low hemoglobin somehow raises arterial PO2 but then blocks oxygen from leaving the plasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen content depends on how much hemoglobin is present, not just on PO2. With fewer grams of hemoglobin, each unit of blood carries fewer milliliters of O2 even though PO2 (and therefore saturation) is normal, so delivery drops. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"Each hemoglobin molecule can transport four O2 molecules. Which structural feature accounts for this?","choices":["Four carbonic anhydrase sites that each convert one O2 for transport","Four globin chains that each dissolve one O2 into the red-cell cytoplasm","Four disulfide bonds that each trap a single dissolved oxygen molecule","Four heme groups, each with one iron atom that reversibly binds one O2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: adult hemoglobin has four globin chains, each with a heme/iron that reversibly binds one O2, so four O2 per molecule. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"Anemia is fundamentally a state in which...","choices":["the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity is reduced below normal","the white blood cell count falls below the normal range","arterial oxygen partial pressure is always markedly low","the blood carries too much oxygen for the tissues to use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anemia means a deficiency of functional hemoglobin (from too few red cells or too little hemoglobin per cell), which lowers the amount of oxygen the blood can carry and deliver to tissues, regardless of the specific cause. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 20-21"},{"q":"A hematocrit of 42% tells you which of the following about a whole-blood sample?","choices":["Red cells occupy about 42% of whole-blood volume, the remainder being plasma","Plasma proteins account for close to 42% of the blood's osmotic pressure","About 42% of circulating hemoglobin is currently saturated with oxygen","Hemoglobin makes up roughly 42% of the mass of each individual red cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: hematocrit is the packed cell (mostly RBC) volume as a percent of whole blood, normally ~40-45%. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"In polycythemia the oxygen-carrying capacity rises, yet the condition can impair circulation because...","choices":["the plasma volume expands and dilutes the hemoglobin","the kidneys stop producing erythropoietin entirely","the extra red cells cannot bind any oxygen at all as it typically occurs here","the accompanying rise in blood viscosity increases resistance to flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Although more red cells raise oxygen-carrying capacity, the higher hematocrit sharply increases blood viscosity; the thicker blood resists flow and raises the load on the heart, which can offset or outweigh the benefit of extra oxygen capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Erythropoietin secretion is governed by a negative feedback loop in which...","choices":["the liver senses red cell count and sets erythropoietin directly","erythropoietin rises steadily regardless of oxygen levels under most normal conditions","high tissue oxygen raises erythropoietin to make more red cells","low tissue oxygen raises erythropoietin, and restored oxygen delivery lowers it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When tissue oxygen delivery falls, the kidneys secrete more erythropoietin, stimulating red cell production; as the added red cells restore oxygen delivery, the stimulus subsides and erythropoietin falls, closing the loop and keeping red cell mass appropriate. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"All blood cell lines ultimately arise from...","choices":["endothelial cells lining the marrow sinusoidal vasculature","pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow","mature lymphocytes that dedifferentiate in the spleen","circulating monocytes that divide within the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every blood cell lineage — red cells, all white cells, and platelets — derives from pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells in the marrow, which give rise to committed progenitors under the influence of specific growth factors. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"When senescent red cells are broken down, most of their iron is...","choices":["converted into bilirubin so it can be safely stored within the liver","excreted unchanged in the bile as the body's main iron-removal route","recycled by macrophages and returned to the marrow for new hemoglobin","lost through the kidneys and must be fully replaced by diet each day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system phagocytize old red cells, salvage the iron, and return it (via transferrin) to the marrow, so the body reuses iron efficiently rather than relying on daily intake. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Adequate red cell production depends on vitamin B12, whose absorption in turn requires...","choices":["erythropoietin that is produced mainly by the kidneys","intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric parietal cells","pancreatic amylase acting in the duodenum","bile salts released from the gallbladder under these conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin B12 must bind intrinsic factor, made by gastric parietal cells, to be absorbed in the terminal ileum; loss of intrinsic factor (as in atrophic gastritis) causes B12 deficiency and the impaired red-cell maturation of pernicious anemia. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17; GI B12 slides"},{"q":"Chronic hypoxia raises red-cell production largely through which mechanism?","choices":["Stabilized HIF-1 drives kidney erythropoietin output, boosting marrow erythropoiesis","Direct hypoxic stimulation of the marrow stem cells acting independently of any hormone","Increased hepatic transferrin release that accelerates hemoglobin assembly","Sympathetic signaling to the spleen that releases stored mature red cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1, which increases renal erythropoietin, stimulating marrow production of red cells. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"Formation of the platelet plug follows the sequence of...","choices":["aggregation first, then adhesion, and finally activation under these conditions","adhesion to the injured wall, activation, then aggregation into a plug","conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin before any platelets act","dissolution of the vessel wall to release clotting factors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Platelets first adhere to exposed collagen at the injury (aided by von Willebrand factor), then become activated and release mediators such as ADP and thromboxane A2, which recruit and cause more platelets to aggregate, building the primary hemostatic plug. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-34"},{"q":"The immediate vascular spasm after a vessel is cut is produced by a combination of...","choices":["local myogenic contraction, nervous reflexes, and platelet-released factors","a rise in blood pressure that forces the vessel closed","erythropoietin acting on the vessel wall during this particular process in the tissues","the fibrin meshwork physically squeezing the vessel shut"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vascular spasm arises from several sources: direct myogenic contraction of the injured vessel, local and nervous reflexes triggered by pain and injury, and vasoconstrictor substances (like thromboxane A2 and serotonin) released by platelets; more severe trauma produces stronger spasm. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34-37"},{"q":"During the platelet response to injury, dense-granule contents such as serotonin contribute by...","choices":["dissolving the forming clot to keep the vessel open","converting fibrinogen into fibrin at the wound","promoting vasoconstriction that reinforces the initial vascular spasm","carrying oxygen to the injured tissue under most conditions in the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Activated platelets release serotonin and thromboxane A2 from their granules; these promote vasoconstriction, reinforcing the vascular spasm that reduces blood flow through the injured vessel while the plug and clot form. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-37"},{"q":"On healthy, intact endothelium, what normally limits inappropriate clot formation?","choices":["Rapid platelet turnover that clears activated factors before they can act","Continuous low-level plasmin release that digests any fibrin as it forms","High local calcium that keeps the clotting factors in their inactive states","A smooth glycocalyx plus thrombomodulin that diverts thrombin toward protein C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the smooth glycocalyx repels platelets/factors, and thrombomodulin binds thrombin to activate protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 48"},{"q":"When platelets contact exposed collagen, they become activated and...","choices":["absorb calcium so it cannot participate in the clotting cascade during this particular process","release plasmin that immediately begins dissolving the clot as it forms","secrete erythropoietin that stimulates the marrow to replace them","release ADP and thromboxane A2 that recruit and activate additional platelets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Activated platelets undergo the release reaction, secreting ADP and thromboxane A2; these recruit and activate more platelets, creating positive feedback that builds the platelet plug at the injury site. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 34"},{"q":"Antithrombin III limits clotting by...","choices":["supplying vitamin K for new factor synthesis","inactivating thrombin and other activated clotting factors","stimulating the platelets to aggregate together much faster","cross-linking fibrin to strengthen the clot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antithrombin III is a natural anticoagulant that binds and neutralizes thrombin and several other activated serine-protease factors, restraining the cascade and helping confine clot formation to the site of injury (its action is greatly accelerated by heparin). — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 48"},{"q":"Once fibrin strands form, factor XIII strengthens the clot by...","choices":["activating platelets to release additional thromboxane A2 locally","dissolving excess fibrin to limit the clot's overall size","cross-linking adjacent fibrin strands into a stable meshwork","converting still more fibrinogen into new fibrin strands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Activated fibrin-stabilizing factor (factor XIII) forms covalent cross-links between fibrin strands, converting the initially loose fibrin into a strong, stable three-dimensional meshwork that resists breakdown. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Von Willebrand factor contributes to primary hemostasis mainly by...","choices":["cleaving fibrinogen into the fibrin strands that anchor the platelet mass","bridging platelets to exposed collagen so they adhere at the injury site","converting prothrombin to thrombin on the surface of activated platelets","triggering vascular smooth muscle spasm to narrow the damaged vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: von Willebrand factor binds platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen, promoting adhesion of the plug. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 34"},{"q":"Vitamin K is required for the liver to make functional prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X because it...","choices":["cleaves the inactive factor precursors into their active protease forms","enables carboxylation of glutamate residues needed for calcium binding","supplies the phospholipid surface where these clotting factors are assembled","transports the finished factors from hepatocytes into the circulating plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: vitamin K is the cofactor for carboxylating glutamate residues, allowing these factors to bind calcium and function. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 54"},{"q":"After a clot forms, clot retraction occurs as platelets...","choices":["release erythropoietin to replace lost red cells","convert fibrin back into soluble fibrinogen during this particular process","contract and pull the fibrin strands together, drawing wound edges closer","dissolve the fibrin to reopen the vessel immediately"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Platelets trapped in the clot contract, pulling on the fibrin meshwork; this retracts the clot, squeezes out serum, and draws the edges of the damaged vessel closer together, aiding healing. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-43"},{"q":"A very low platelet count tends to cause bleeding because platelets are needed to...","choices":["carry the oxygen required for wound healing during this particular process","form the initial plug and support the reactions of the clotting cascade","dissolve clots so blood can keep flowing freely","produce the antibodies that fight wound infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Platelets form the primary hemostatic plug at sites of injury and provide the phospholipid surface on which clotting reactions assemble; when their number is too low, both plug formation and clot generation are impaired, producing a bleeding tendency. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-34"},{"q":"Heparin acts as an anticoagulant chiefly by...","choices":["supplying the vitamin K needed to make clotting factors","directly cross-linking fibrin to trap the clotting factors","greatly enhancing the activity of antithrombin against thrombin","stimulating platelets to form a stronger plug during this process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heparin binds antithrombin III and dramatically accelerates its ability to inactivate thrombin and other activated clotting factors; this potentiation is why heparin rapidly reduces clotting, and it is normally released in small amounts by mast cells and basophils. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 48"},{"q":"To keep a donated blood sample from clotting during storage, an agent is added that removes or binds calcium; this works because calcium is...","choices":["the trigger that begins the intrinsic pathway","the enzyme that directly cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin","required at several steps of the clotting cascade","needed only to dissolve clots after they form"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium ions (factor IV) are needed for multiple reactions of the coagulation cascade, so binding or removing calcium (for example, with citrate) prevents the cascade from proceeding and keeps stored blood from clotting. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-47"},{"q":"Fibrinolysis is triggered when plasminogen activators such as tPA...","choices":["convert plasminogen trapped in the clot into active plasmin","stimulate platelets to build a larger clot during this process","convert plasmin back into inactive plasminogen","cross-link fibrin strands to strengthen the clot"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) and similar activators convert clot-bound plasminogen into plasmin, the enzyme that digests fibrin; this gradually dissolves the clot once the vessel has healed and restores normal flow. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-48"},{"q":"Platelets and healthy endothelium exert opposing influences on clotting because platelets release thromboxane A2 while intact endothelium releases...","choices":["additional thromboxane A2 to reinforce aggregation","prostacyclin, which inhibits platelet aggregation and dilates the vessel","fibrinogen to accelerate clot formation on its surface","erythropoietin to stimulate red cell production during this particular process"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thromboxane A2 from activated platelets promotes aggregation and vasoconstriction, whereas intact endothelium releases prostacyclin, which inhibits platelet aggregation and dilates the vessel; the balance keeps clotting localized to injury sites. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-34, 48"}]};
